  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1255 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThe upper low exits northeast to the Dakotas and central Canada \nTuesday flattening the upper high from eastern NM to central and \nsouthern Appalachians and strengthening to 597 dam at 500 mb over \nthe Ozarks and central Mississippi River Valley. The monsoon plume \nand thus better diurnal shower and storm coverage will remain across \nwestern and central NM similar to Labor Day. CAMs do show a better \ncoverage across the Sacramento Mountains Tuesday compared to Labor \nDay. The 597 dam 500 mb upper high looks to pivot back west to over \nNew Mexico Wednesday due to the trough axis south of the Canadian \nlow moving across the north central U.S. This trough looks to send a \nweak backdoor front through northeast and central NM ending up along \nthe east slopes of the Sangre de Cristo and Sandia and Manzano \nMountains by midday Wednesday. This will result in a boost of \nmoisture and lift and thus a better coverage of showers and storms \nacross this part of the state. \n\nThe upper high continues shifting west to southern NV, western AZ, \nand southern CA weakening to 594 to 596 dam at 500 mb while the \nbackdoor front mixes out across eastern NM Thursday. Overall storm \ncoverage on Thursday does look lower than Wednesday as some drier \nair tries to move in from the north. Forecast confidence Friday into \nnext weekend decreases rapidly as guidance differs on how quickly \nthe upper high over the Desert SW breaks down ahead of a new upper \nlow entering the Pacific NW. The ECMWF breaks down the upper high \nFriday, while the GFS, AI-GFS, and Canadian shift the 594 to 595 dam \nupper high back east over New Mexico. For the weekend, the AI-GFS \nand Canadian keep the upper high squarely overhead Saturday before \nshifting east next Sunday. The GFS shift the upper high over west \nTexas Saturday before shifting east to the south central U.S. Sunday \nas the upper low moves across the northern Rockies. Finally, the \nECMWF has the upper low and associated troughing across the western \nU.S. more dominant next weekend. The ECMWF scenario would result in \na higher coverage of showers and storms, especially across the \nhigher elevations, during this period. The GFS would hold the higher \nshower and storm coverage until Sunday and the AI-GFS and Canadian \nwould have a lower coverage of showers and storms across the higher \nterrain. Right now, the ensemble cluster guidance shows a slight \nlean (58% to 42%) to a mixture of the ECMWF and GFS scenarios. Highs \nthrough Friday will be in the upper 80s to mid 90s across lower \nelevations of western and central NM and mid to upper 90s across \neastern NM which is slightly above average for early to mid \nSeptember. Some potential cooling next weekend if the more dominant \nupper low/troughing scenario pans out.",
